# strings (step 9.4b): Go's package; Builder keeps its address as a uintptr. (The iterator functions, Lines and the Seq ones, are in since step 7.10.)

@note Builder remembers its address as a uintptr, not as a *Builder: in gocpp a pointer field is a counted reference, so Go's `b.addr = b` made every Builder own itself and never free its buffer (a reference cycle, ADR-004). Same checks and the same panic; no strings.Builder leaks.

@file builder.go
@why Go's field `addr *Builder` is a reference to the Builder itself: with reference counting the Builder (and its buffer) then lives forever. A uintptr detects the copy by value just as well (a copy has another address).
@replace
	addr *Builder // of receiver, to detect copies by value
@with
	addr uintptr // address of the receiver, to detect copies by value (a *Builder would be a reference cycle)
@end
@why copyCheck compares addresses instead of pointers; the escape-analysis workaround (abi.NoEscape) is not needed for a uintptr.
@replace
	if b.addr == nil {
		// This hack works around a failing of Go's escape analysis
		// that was causing b to escape and be heap allocated.
		// See issue 23382.
		// TODO: once issue 7921 is fixed, this should be reverted to
		// just "b.addr = b".
		b.addr = (*Builder)(abi.NoEscape(unsafe.Pointer(b)))
	} else if b.addr != b {
@with
	if b.addr == 0 {
		b.addr = uintptr(unsafe.Pointer(b))
	} else if b.addr != uintptr(unsafe.Pointer(b)) {
@end
@why abi is not used any more.
@replace
	"internal/abi"
	"internal/bytealg"
@with
	"internal/bytealg"
@end
@replace
	b.addr = nil
@with
	b.addr = 0
@end
